- the newest relevant dated files and decision-log entries
- `docs/project-state/repository-relationship.md`
- `docs/PROXYWAR_REPOSITORY_RELATIONSHIP.md`

Also scan relevant internal docs under `docs/` for the task. This repo contains brainstorming and older product docs; treat `docs/project-state/` as the durable decision source unless another doc is confirmed there. Tag claims `[repo/file verified]` vs `[uncertain / needs confirmation]`.

Separate repository truth, local artifact truth, and live hosted truth. Verify
live Coworld/Softmax claims read-only when they materially affect a decision;
never infer a live package, league binding, ranking, or runtime result from
local files alone.

## Codex Operating Layer

- `.codex/config.toml` registers the bounded specialist roles and concurrency.
- `.codex/agents/` contains Codex-native role charters.
- `.codex/skills/` contains reusable catch-up, Coworld-status, and readiness workflows.
- `.codex/hooks.json` and `.codex/hooks/` provide local safety checks.
- `.claude/` and `CLAUDE*.md` are legacy reference, not the active control plane.
- Codex reads project instructions at task start. Restart a task after changing
  `AGENTS.md`, project config, role definitions, skills, or hooks when the new
  operating layer must be guaranteed for that task.

## Engineering Rules

- Keep `src/core` deterministic.
- `src/core` uses seeded randomness and avoids floating-point simulation math.
  It already has selected dependencies, but no new dependency may introduce
  nondeterminism, provider calls, or simulation networking without explicit
  architecture review. Every `src/core` change needs relevant focused tests;
  simulation-behavior tests should use the real `tests/util/Setup.ts` harness
  rather than mocks, while schemas/config/loaders may use their focused suites.
- Do not put LLM, Codex, OpenAI, external-agent HTTP, or network-provider logic in `src/core`. (Config/map loading over `fetch` in the existing loaders is the allowed exception — the simulation is what must stay deterministic.)
- Agents must choose existing `LegalAction.id` values only.
- Do not allow external agents or LLMs to emit raw OpenFront intents.
- Preserve the canonical path: `AgentObservation -> LegalAction[] -> AgentDecision -> AgentDecisionValidator -> AgentRunner -> GameServer`.
- Do not create duplicate runners, validators, action schemas, or direct-intent agent paths.
- Mock/rule paths stay default for tests. Codex, OpenAI, and external HTTP providers stay opt-in. (Codex CLI is being deprecated for the in-house agent; prefer a Claude-CLI house brain when one exists.)

OpenFront simulation flow is client action -> validated Intent -> server Turn
relay -> client Core worker -> Execution -> `executeNextTick()` -> GameUpdates.
Intents and wire messages are validated from `src/core/Schemas.ts`. Preserve this
flow unless a durable architecture decision explicitly changes it.

All user-visible UI text must use `translateText()` and add its English entry to
`resources/lang/en.json`. Do not edit other translation files; Crowdin owns them.

Use an explicit plan and an independent reviewer before risky changes under
`src/core/**` or to `AgentRunner.ts`, `AgentDecisionValidator.ts`,
`LegalActionBuilder.ts`, `AgentObservationBuilder.ts`,
`AgentPlannerExecutor.ts`, or `AgentDemoHub.ts`. Review is mandatory for edits
to `AgentPlannerExecutor.ts` and `AgentDemoHub.ts`.

## Common Commands

- Install: `npm run inst` (`npm ci --ignore-scripts`; do NOT use `npm install`)
- Typecheck: `npm exec -- tsc --noEmit`
- Lint: `npm run lint`
- Test: `npm test`
- Single test: `npx vitest path/to/test.ts --run` (or pass a test-name pattern)
- Build dev: `npm run build-dev`
- Build prod: `npm run build-prod`
- Main local dev: `npm run dev`
- Coworld certify: `cd coworld-adapter && npm run certify`
- Coworld local episode: `cd coworld-adapter && npm run run:episode`

Historical/maintenance-only unless explicitly reactivated: `agent:demo-server`,
`agent:closed-beta`, `agent:public-readiness:strict`,
`agent:hosted-beta:readiness`, `agent:external-agent:dry-run`, and
`agent:benchmark:external-full`.

Use focused tests for narrow changes, then run the relevant Coworld certification,
episode, agent-evaluation, or replay path before calling Coworld-facing work
complete.

## Prioritization

Current priority order:

1. Coworld package, hosted-runtime, league, replay, and result-contract reliability.
2. Keystone competitive performance and honest degradation telemetry.
3. Fast, reproducible Coworld evaluation against the actual hosted opponent class.
4. Softmax-facing integration clarity, evidence, and operational handoff.
5. Watchability only where it improves Coworld evaluation or league adoption.

Do not spend active project time on private-beta onboarding, generic frontend
polish, starter-agent growth, or broad public-launch work unless it directly
supports Coworld or the competitive agent.

## Collaboration Model

Use one user-facing Control thread. The Control thread owns prioritization,
integration, verification, and durable project-state updates. Spawn bounded
subagents for parallel Coworld integration, agent strategy, investigation, and
review work; do not require the operator to coordinate separate specialist
threads.

Use subagents only for concrete, independently verifiable work. Control reviews
their evidence and remains responsible for the final result. Keep nesting at
one level and at most four concurrent threads unless the operator changes the
resource budget.

## Autonomy

Codex (or any delegated coding agent) may decide and execute autonomously when the work is reversible and within the existing architecture: docs, tests, bug fixes, UI copy, readiness scripts, artifact/report fixes, canonical behavior-module improvements, local branches, staging, and local commits.

The agent must pause for real blockers: credentials or login approval, unavailable external accounts, destructive operations, private secrets, billing/domain decisions, npm/package ownership decisions, or product direction changes that cannot be inferred. Never read or print `.env*`, `proprietary/`, `~/.ssh/`, OAuth stores, tokens, auth databases, or other private auth material; check only whether a required variable is present. Codex may read the installed skill/config metadata under `~/.codex/` that its own system instructions explicitly require, but must not inspect credentials or auth stores there.

Gated outward actions (do only when the operator asked in-conversation): any git push, force-push, branch deletion / history rewrite, deploys, `npm publish`, Coworld hosted upload/submit/publish, sending external messages, and deleting replay/benchmark/artifact files. Land future changes on a branch off `main` using the `codex/` prefix.

Do not ask the user to do work the agent can reasonably do itself.

## Storage Lifecycle

- Keep the fixed free-space policy: new worktrees and dependency installs need
  the 25 GiB floor plus a 2 GiB pending-growth reserve; backups and heavyweight
  evaluations need 25 GiB plus their pending estimate; 15 GiB is the
  bounded-write floor; 10 GiB plus pending replay bytes is the immutable mirror
  reserve. `PROXYWAR_ALLOW_LOW_DISK_HEAVY_WRITE=1` may relax evaluations and
  measured backups from 25 GiB to 15 GiB, never worktree or dependency creation.
- Create shell-managed ProxyWar worktrees only through
  `/Users/claude/Documents/proxywar_main/.codex/worktree-lifecycle.mjs create`.
  It writes the owner lease before checkout creation. For a worktree allocated
  internally by Codex Desktop, run `register` immediately after task start.
  Codex tasks use `--owner-kind=codex --owner-id="$CODEX_THREAD_ID"`; heartbeat
  before and after long work, and explicitly `complete` only when the owner is
  done.
- Pin worktrees that are active handoffs or contain evidence. A completed tree
  receives a 72-hour TTL (24 hours only for explicitly temporary trees).
- Never call `git worktree remove`, `git worktree prune`, or raw filesystem
  deletion directly. The lifecycle controller is the sole removal path. It
  rechecks registration, explicit completion, TTL, pins, path containment,
  process CWDs, open files, tracked/untracked state, ignored evidence, and
  branch/HEAD preservation, then removes at most two trees or 2 GiB per run.
- Unregistered legacy trees are audit-only. Never auto-adopt or auto-delete
  them. Do not use mutable shared `node_modules` symlinks; share npm's
  lockfile-keyed content cache and install dependencies only where a task
  actually needs them.
- The external `Crucial X9` SSD is bound through
  `/Users/claude/Library/Application Support/ProxyWar/bin/proxywar-external-storage.mjs`.
  Agents must use its lease controller before writing and must read
  `/Users/claude/Library/Application Support/ProxyWar/storage/external-storage/AGENT_USAGE.md`.
  Use it only for cold verified archives, ordinary artifact/evaluation output,
  archive staging, and leased scratch data. Keep a 100 GiB external reserve.
- Never place Git repositories/worktrees, `node_modules`, dependency stores,
  Docker/Colima data, executable build trees, the active frontend, live league
  mirror/replay feed, secrets, or proprietary material on the exFAT SSD. Never
  substitute an external symlink for an archived local path. External archive
  apply remains operator-gated because it removes the verified local copy.
- The scheduled host controls are installed under
  `/Users/claude/Library/Application Support/ProxyWar/bin`; their atomic
  watermark, lifecycle audit, lease, and job-state records live in the adjacent
  `storage/` directory. Keep launchd control scripts outside `Documents` to
  avoid macOS privacy isolation.
- Storage maintenance never deletes artifacts, outputs, replays, benchmarks,
  results, backups, Docker state, branches, dirty trees, or unknown ignored
  material.

